## The idea

Walk both trees at the same positions. Both present → a **new** node holding the sum. Only one present → a **copy** of that subtree. Building new nodes keeps both inputs unchanged.

## Step by step

1. Both null → null.
2. One null → copy the other.
3. Both present → new node (a + b), then merge the left children and the right children.

## Worked example

**Input:** a = [1,3,2,5], b = [2,1,3,null,4,null,7]

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| roots | 1 + 2 = 3 |
| left | 3 + 1 = 4; its children 5 (only a) and 4 (only b) |
| right | 2 + 3 = 5; right child 7 (only b) |

**Result:** [3, 4, 5, 5, 4, null, 7]

## Cost

O(n + m) time and memory.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

