// Builds a NEW tree, so neither input changes (a caller may still be using them).
public static class MergeTrees {
    public static TreeNode Merge(TreeNode a, TreeNode b) {
        if (a == null && b == null) return null;
        if (a == null) return Copy(b);
        if (b == null) return Copy(a);
        var node = new TreeNode(a.Value + b.Value);
        node.Left = Merge(a.Left, b.Left);
        node.Right = Merge(a.Right, b.Right);
        return node;
    }

    private static TreeNode Copy(TreeNode t) {
        if (t == null) return null;
        var node = new TreeNode(t.Value);
        node.Left = Copy(t.Left);
        node.Right = Copy(t.Right);
        return node;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Mutate or copy?**

Ask. Mutating a is O(overlap) and allocation-free; copying is safe for callers who keep the inputs.

**Q: Complexity?**

O(n + m) time and memory for the copy.

**Q: Shared state?**

Read-only inputs — safe to merge concurrently from several threads.

**Q: Very deep trees.**

An explicit stack of node triples (a, b, out).

